[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unsafe Pointer and Memory Layout in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go is a strongly typed, memory-safe language by design. However, low-level system programming, high-performance serialization, and interfacing with C libraries occasionally require bypassing Go\'s type system. This is achieved using the unsafe package and unsafe.Pointer."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Memory Layout and Alignment"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before using unsafe operations, you must understand how Go structures memory. Variables are stored in memory aligned to their size boundaries. Padding bytes are often inserted by the compiler to ensure proper CPU alignment, which optimizes memory access speeds."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Alignment rules dictate that a variable of size N must be allocated at a memory address divisible by N."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Struct fields are ordered in memory matching their declaration order, subject to padding."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"What is unsafe.Pointer?"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"An unsafe.Pointer is a special pointer type that can hold the memory address of any variable. Normal Go pointers are strict; you cannot assign a *int32 to a *int64 variable. An unsafe.Pointer bypasses this restriction, allowing a pointer of one type to be converted into a pointer of another type."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"unsafe"
)

func main() {
	var x int32 = 42
	// Convert *int32 to unsafe.Pointer, then to *int64
	var p *int64 = (*int64)(unsafe.Pointer(&x))
	// WARNING: This causes memory corruption/reads out of bounds
	fmt.Println(*p)
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Valid Uses of unsafe.Pointer"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Go runtime specification explicitly defines safe patterns for using unsafe.Pointer. Violating these patterns can break garbage collection and lead to undefined behavior."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conversion between a *T and unsafe.Pointer."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Converting an unsafe.Pointer to a uintptr and back, enabling pointer arithmetic."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conversion of unsafe.Pointer to reflect.SliceHeader or reflect.StringHeader."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Pointer Arithmetic Example"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To access individual fields or array elements dynamically, you must cast an unsafe.Pointer to a uintptr, perform arithmetic operations (adding byte offsets), and cast it back to an unsafe.Pointer."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"unsafe"
)

type Foo struct {
	a int8
	b int32
}

func main() {
	foo := Foo{a: 1, b: 200}

	// Get pointer to field \'b\' using unsafe offset
	ptrB := (*int32)(unsafe.Pointer(uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&foo)) + unsafe.Offsetof(foo.b)))

	fmt.Println(*ptrB) // Outputs 200
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Safety Warnings"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Never store a uintptr value in a variable and keep it across function calls. If the garbage collector runs, it will not recognize uintptr as a valid memory reference, potentially freeing or moving the underlying memory while your program still holds the address."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minimize the use of unsafe and isolate it to well-tested packages."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always run tests with the Go race detector enabled (-race)."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be aware that memory layouts can change across different Go versions and architectures."}]
